  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>This area listing groups three campsites between Cagnes-sur-Mer and Villeneuve-Loubet. Two beaches where dogs are admitted are nearby: l'Hippodrome in Cagnes-sur-Mer (dogs between groyne 1 and groyne 2, may be off the lead if supervised, per the metropolitan tourist office) and Vaugrenier in Villeneuve-Loubet (the only beach in the commune that accepts animals, open daily in 2026, closed from midnight to 6 am, no barbecues, per the local tourist office). The town of Cagnes-sur-Mer states on its website that a bathing zone is reserved for dogs on Hippodrome beach, between groynes 1 and 2, and that it has created dog areas in town, open 24/7 and mostly with drinking-water fountains and shade.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you book</h2>
      <p>Conditions vary from one campsite to another. Camping La Vieille Ferme (Villeneuve-Loubet): its official site says animals are accepted on a lead with an up-to-date vaccination booklet, except category 1 and 2 dogs; closed from 16 October 2026 to 1 February 2027. Camping de l'Hippodrome (Villeneuve-Loubet): the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur tourism listing says animals are accepted "depending on category", with a surcharge, and the grounds are closed at night; no amount is published. Camping Le Colombier (Cagnes-sur-Mer, open 1 April to 30 September): its rules state animals are admitted out of season only, dogs on a lead and never left alone, no animals in the mobile homes, vaccination certificate and identification at reception, and category 1 and 2 dogs forbidden.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: phone the campsite you choose before booking; the surcharge amount at l'Hippodrome is not published and Le Colombier admits dogs out of season only.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Marina Baie des Anges, with its unmistakable architecture, is worth a look at the end of a walk on Vaugrenier beach.</p>
